+---
+id: dl-001-key-tasks-by-a-sequential-id-resolve-title-or-id-in-lookups
+type: decision-log
+title: "Key tasks by a sequential id, resolve title-or-id in lookups"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Tasks needed a stable identifier that survives a rename, since the list used to key its internal
+`Map` by title — renaming a task meant deleting and re-inserting it under the new key, with nothing
+for the app to hold onto across that change. `r3-no-clock-or-randomness` forbids the domain from
+reading the clock or generating random values, including random identifiers, so the id could not be
+a UUID or a timestamp-derived value.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Each `TaskList` instance keeps a private counter (`nextId`, starting at 1) and assigns the next
+  integer, as a string, to every task `add`s. The counter only advances; ids are never reused, even
+  after `remove`. This is deterministic (same sequence of calls always yields the same ids) and
+  satisfies the no-randomness directive, since it is not random.
+- The list's internal `Map` is now keyed by `id` instead of by `title`, since the id is the one key
+  guaranteed not to change.
+- `find`, and everything built on it (`complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag`, `remove`), now
+  accepts either a title or an id: it checks titles first, falling back to id. Title-first keeps
+  every existing call site working unchanged; the fallback is additive.
+- `get(id)` is a separate, id-only lookup, as requested, returning `Result<Task>` like the list's
+  other methods, with a `not-found` error for an unknown id.
+- `createTask` (`src/domain/task.ts`) takes the id as a parameter rather than generating one, so the
+  domain still never invents an identifier on its own; the list, which already owns the mutable
+  counter, is the one place that decides what the next id is.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `Task` gained a required `id` field, which changes its shape wherever it's constructed or
+  compared (tests updated accordingly), but no existing method signature changed meaning: every
+  method that used to take a title still accepts one and behaves the same.
+- A task's id is scoped to the `TaskList` instance that created it; there is no cross-list identity.
+- Because lookups try title first, a title that happens to look like an id (e.g. a task literally
+  titled "1") still resolves by title, not by colliding with another task's id.
