+---
+id: task-001-add-stable-task-ids
+type: task
+title: "Add stable task ids"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Give each task a stable identifier that keeps working after the task is renamed:
+
+- `TaskList.add` returns a task with a unique `id`.
+- `TaskList` gets a `get(id)` method, returning a `Result<Task>` like the other methods.
+- `find`, `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag` and `remove` accept either a title or an id.
+
+## What I did
+
+- `src/domain/task.ts`: added `id: string` to `Task`; `createTask` now takes the id as a second
+  parameter instead of inventing one, so the domain still never generates an identifier itself
+  (per `r3-no-clock-or-randomness`) — the list below hands it a sequential one.
+- `src/domain/list.ts`: `createTaskList` now stores tasks in its `Map` by `id` instead of by title
+  (titles can change; ids must not), and assigns ids from a private, monotonically increasing
+  counter (`nextId`) local to each list instance — deterministic, no clock or randomness.
+  - Added `get(id)`, looking up by id only.
+  - `find` (and so `complete`, `reopen`, `rename`, `tag`, `untag`, `remove`, all built on it) now
+    resolves its argument against titles first, then ids, so every existing call site that passes a
+    title keeps working unchanged.
+  - `rename`'s duplicate-title check now compares against the task's current title (found via
+    `titleOrId`) rather than the raw input, which is equivalent for title-based calls and correct
+    for id-based ones.
+- `test/list.test.ts`: updated the existing "adds a task" assertion to allow for the new `id` field,
+  and added tests for: unique ids on add, `get` success/not-found, an id resolving the task after a
+  rename, and every title-accepting method also accepting an id.
+
+Ran the new tests red (failing against the old implementation) before implementing, then green
+after. `npm test`: 11/11 passing.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+None; the public API addition is additive (titles keep working everywhere they did before).
